Certainly! Here's a JavaScript function that switches the order of adjectives in a sentence:

```javascript
function switchAdjectives(sentence) {
  const words = sentence.split(" ");
  const adjectives = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
    if (words[i].match(/^ad\w+$/)) {
      adjectives.push(words[i]);
    }
  }

  if (adjectives.length > 1) {
    const firstAdjective = adjectives.shift();
    const lastAdjective = adjectives.pop();

    adjectives.splice(1, 0, lastAdjective);
    adjectives.unshift(firstAdjective);

    const newSentence = adjectives.join(" ") + " " + words.slice(1).join(" ");
    return newSentence;
  } else {
    return sentence;
  }
}

const inputSentence = "Beautiful green trees are in the park";
const outputSentence = switchAdjectives(inputSentence);
console.log(outputSentence); // Output: Green beautiful trees are in the park
```

This function splits the input sentence into an array of words, then filters out all the adjectives using a regular expression. It then reorders the adjectives and combines them with the rest of the sentence to create the output sentence. If there are no adjectives in the sentence, it simply returns the input sentence unchanged.

